## Solution

A function $u$ is **harmonic** iff $\Delta u = u_{xx} + u_{yy} = 0$.

Compute the second partials of $u(x,y) = ax^3 + by^3 + cxy^2 + dx^2y$:

$$u_x = 3ax^2 + 2dxy + cy^2, \qquad u_{xx} = 6ax + 2dy,$$
$$u_y = 3by^2 + 2cxy + dx^2, \qquad u_{yy} = 6by + 2cx.$$

Hence

$$\Delta u = (6a + 2c)\,x + (2d + 6b)\,y.$$

For this to vanish for **all** $x, y$, both coefficients must be zero:

$$6a + 2c = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; c = -3a, \qquad 2d + 6b = 0 \;\Longrightarrow\; d = -3b.$$

So the most general harmonic $u$ of the given form is

$$u = a\,x^3 + b\,y^3 - 3a\,xy^2 - 3b\,x^2y = a\,(x^3 - 3xy^2) + b\,(y^3 - 3x^2y).$$

Recall that with $z = x + iy$,

$$z^3 = (x^3 - 3xy^2) + i\,(3x^2y - y^3),$$

so $\operatorname{Re}(z^3) = x^3 - 3xy^2$ and $\operatorname{Im}(z^3) = 3x^2y - y^3 = -(y^3 - 3x^2y)$. Therefore

$$u = a\,\operatorname{Re}(z^3) - b\,\operatorname{Im}(z^3) = \operatorname{Re}\!\big((a + ib)\,z^3\big).$$

Since $(a+ib)z^3$ is entire, its imaginary part is a harmonic conjugate of its real part. Thus

$$v = \operatorname{Im}\!\big((a+ib)\,z^3\big) = a\,\operatorname{Im}(z^3) + b\,\operatorname{Re}(z^3) = a\,(3x^2y - y^3) + b\,(x^3 - 3xy^2),$$

up to an arbitrary real additive constant.

**Verification via Cauchy–Riemann.** With $c = -3a,\; d = -3b$:

$$u_x = 3ax^2 - 3ay^2 - 6bxy, \qquad u_y = 3by^2 - 6axy - 3bx^2.$$

From $v_x = -u_y = 3bx^2 + 6axy - 3by^2$, integrating in $x$:

$$v = bx^3 + 3ax^2y - 3bxy^2 + g(y).$$

Then $v_y = 3ax^2 - 6bxy + g'(y)$, and matching with $v_y = u_x = 3ax^2 - 3ay^2 - 6bxy$ gives $g'(y) = -3ay^2$, so $g(y) = -ay^3 + \text{const}$. Hence

$$v = b\,(x^3 - 3xy^2) + a\,(3x^2y - y^3) + \text{const},$$

confirming the result, and $u + iv = (a+ib)\,z^3 + i\,\text{const}$ is holomorphic.

## Answer

The function is harmonic iff $\boxed{c = -3a \text{ and } d = -3b}$ (with $a, b$ arbitrary real numbers). The most general harmonic $u$ is

$$u = a(x^3 - 3xy^2) + b(y^3 - 3x^2y) = \operatorname{Re}\!\big((a+ib)\,z^3\big),$$

and a harmonic conjugate is

$$v = a(3x^2y - y^3) + b(x^3 - 3xy^2) + \text{const} = \operatorname{Im}\!\big((a+ib)\,z^3\big) + \text{const},$$

so that $u + iv = (a+ib)\,z^3 + i\,\text{const}$ is holomorphic.

